        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\n'mini.sessions' is [built to rely on built-in `mksession`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/1cd8d7e8318071eddc536815f5cafe50b012ac29/doc/mini-sessions.txt#L8-L9). This type of behavior is aligned with how it operates. I am not sure about 100% accuracy here, but built-in sessions seem to work reliably only for windows currently displaying normal buffers.\r\n\r\nFor customizing that behavior there are indeed [hooks](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/1cd8d7e8318071eddc536815f5cafe50b012ac29/doc/mini-sessions.txt#L92-L99). As a more drastic but filetype agnostic measure, I'd suggest closing all windows which at the moment of writing contain non-normal buffer. With something like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal close_non_normal_windows = function()\r\n  for _, win_id in ipairs(vim.api.nvim_list_wins()) do\r\n    local buf_id = vim.api.nvim_win_get_buf(win_id)\r\n    if vim.bo[buf_id].buftype ~= '' then vim.api.nvim_win_close(win_id, true) end\r\n  end\r\nend\r\nrequire('mini.sessions').setup({ hooks = { pre = { write = close_non_normal_windows } } })\r\n```\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned.",
